#=GF DR   SO; 0000035; riboswitch;
#=GF DR   GO; 0031419; cobalamin binding;
#=GF RN   [1]
#=GF RM   12787499
#=GF RT   Riboswitches control fundamental biochemical pathways in Bacillus subtilis
#=GF RT   and other bacteria.
#=GF RA   Mandal M, Boese B, Barrick JE, Winkler WC, Breaker RR
#=GF RL   Cell 2003;113:577-586.
#=GF RN   [2]
#=GF RM   12923257
#=GF RT   Regulation of the vitamin B12 metabolism and transport in bacteria by a
#=GF RT   conserved RNA structural element.
#=GF RA   Vitreschak AG, Rodionov DA, Mironov AA, Gelfand MS
#=GF RL   RNA 2003;9:1084-1097.
#=GF RN   [3]
#=GF RM   14704351
#=GF RT   Coenzyme B12 riboswitches are widespread genetic control elements in
#=GF RT   prokaryotes.
#=GF RA   Nahvi A, Barrick JE, Breaker RR
#=GF RL   Nucleic Acids Res 2004;32:143-150.
#=GF RN   [4]
#=GF RM   28483920
#=GF RT   Cobalamin riboswitches exhibit a broad range of ability to discriminate
#=GF RT   between methylcobalamin and adenosylcobalamin.
#=GF RA   Polaski JT, Webster SM, Johnson JE Jr, Batey RT
#=GF RL   J Biol Chem. 2017;292:11650-11658.
#=GF RN   [5]
#=GF RM   23064232
#=GF RT   B12 cofactors directly stabilize an mRNA regulatory switch.
#=GF RA   Johnson JE Jr, Reyes FE, Polaski JT, Batey RT
#=GF RL   Nature. 2012;492:133-137.
#=GF RN   [6]
#=GF RM   23064646
#=GF RT   Structural insights into ligand binding and gene expression control by an
#=GF RT   adenosylcobalamin riboswitch.
#=GF RA   Peselis A, Serganov A
#=GF RL   Nat Struct Mol Biol. 2012;19:1182-1184.
#=GF RN   [7]
#=GF RM   32544228
#=GF RT   Crystal structure of an atypical cobalamin riboswitch reveals RNA
#=GF RT   structural adaptability as basis for promiscuous ligand binding.
#=GF RA   Chan CW, Mondragon A
#=GF RL   Nucleic Acids Res. 2020;48:7569-7583.
#=GF CC   Riboswitches are metabolite binding domains within certain messenger RNAs
#=GF CC   that serve as precision sensors for their corresponding targets.
#=GF CC   Allosteric rearrangement of mRNA structure is mediated by ligand binding,
#=GF CC   and this results in modulation of gene expression [1]. This family
#=GF CC   represents a cobalamin riboswitch (B12-element) which is widely
#=GF CC   distributed in 5' UTRs of vitamin B(12)-related genes in bacteria.
#=GF CC   Cobalamin in the form of adenosylcobalamin (Ado-CBL) is known to repress
#=GF CC   expression of genes for vitamin B(12) biosynthesis and be transported by a
#=GF CC   post-transcriptional regulatory mechanism, which involves direct binding
#=GF CC   of Ado-CBL to 5' UTRs [2]. Cobalamin riboswitch structure was reported in
#=GF CC   PDB:4GMA [5], 4GXY [6] and 6VMY [7]. Cobalamin riboswitch forms a
#=GF CC   junctional structure that form a pocket that recognise adenosylcobalamin
#=GF CC   and is flanked by structural elements that connects by long-range
#=GF CC   interactions. 
#=GF WK   Cobalamin_riboswitch
#=GF SQ   433

